  :adr/context
  "Asked to move CI/CD repo-wide off GitHub Actions and onto murakumo.cloud. Two things turned out to be true before any design was needed.

First, most of the work already exists. `scripts/fleet-ci/` runs commit-triggered gates on the murakumo mac-mini fleet, signs receipts into an append-only ledger, writes commit statuses back, and advances west pins when green — ADR-2607178000 plus ADR-2607255500. Measured 2026-07-30: 17 gate entries, 23 units per tick including 6 PR heads, fanned across 6 capable nodes. Its README lists PR-head verification as not-done, but tick.cljs implements it (line ~962 closes that item explicitly); the README is stale, not the system.

Second, and decisively: **GitHub Actions is not running at all here.** All 16 workflows in com-junkawasaki/root show `failure` on their latest run, and inspecting those runs shows **zero steps recorded and no logs** — the signature of a job that never started, not of a check that failed. gates.edn already recorded the cause on 2026-07-26: Actions billing stopped for com-junkawasaki and gftdcojp, so jobs do not launch. kotoba-lang/kotoba is now failing too.

So the premise of the request is stronger than a platform preference. There is currently no CI. Sixteen red lights have been standing in for gating, and nothing behind them has run for weeks."
  :adr/decision
  "1. The murakumo fleet (`scripts/fleet-ci/`) is the CI/CD plane for this workspace. GitHub Actions is treated as unavailable, not as a second opinion.

2. Port each workflow's **invariant**, never its implementation. A gate script ships inputs and calls the repo's own verifier; two implementations of one invariant let a check pass on one path and fail on the other unnoticed. This is already the rule for repository-roles and manifest-reality gates.

3. **Credentials never reach a node** (existing invariant 3). This splits every workflow rather than moving it wholesale: the part that needs only the source tree becomes a node-side gate; the part needing GitHub API auth stays operator-side in tick.cljs, which already holds tokens and already runs verify-west-pins before advancing a pin.

4. **Never register a gate that starts red.** A permanently-failing check carries no signal — that is the exact mechanism by which the docs-edn defect was reintroduced twice within hours today (ADR-2607300400). A workflow whose invariant is currently violated gets its breakage fixed first, or gets registered with a declared ratchet, but does not get added as a standing red light.

5. `exit 0` is not a pass. Every ported gate asserts a positive summary from the underlying verifier, as jvm-test asserts `Ran N tests`.

6. **Delete the workflows rather than leaving them red.** Sixteen failing checks are worse than none: they read as coverage. `.github/` is removed from this superproject on 2026-07-30. Git history keeps every recipe, and no required status check exists to strand a PR (branch protection is unavailable on this plan). Two capabilities are removed outright and named as such rather than quietly dropped."
  :adr/consequences
  "Coverage is now 18 gate entries. The invariant that pin regressions are checked at all is restored — it had silently not been running.

Branch protection is the remaining cutover: required status checks still name Actions contexts that never report, so they cannot be satisfied and cannot block anything. Switching them to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*` is what actually makes the fleet authoritative, and it is a repo-settings change, not code.

Two capabilities are gone rather than moved: SAST (codeql) and the JupyterLite Pages publish. Both were already not executing, but nothing will restore them automatically — they need an owner decision, recorded in the body.

The fleet is now the only CI plane for this superproject. That also means its coverage gaps are the real ones: nine invariants listed in the body have no home at all until they are ported, and four of those are currently violated.

Trigger latency stays at the 5-minute LaunchAgent poll rather than a webhook. Acceptable for gates; it means a push waits up to 5 minutes for a verdict."
  :adr/body
  "## The evidence that Actions is dead

| check | result |
|---|---|
| workflows in com-junkawasaki/root | 16, all `runs-on: ubuntu-latest` |
| latest run conclusion | **failure on all 16** |
| steps recorded in those jobs | **0** |
| logs for the failed job | **absent** (`log not found`) |
| contrast | kotoba-lang/kotoba latest 3 runs also `failure` |

Zero steps and no logs mean the runner never picked the job up. Combined with the billing note already in gates.edn, this is a startup failure, not a verification failure.

## Workflow → fleet mapping

**Already on the fleet (3).** docs-edn-verify (as `docs-edn-check`, scoped to `90-docs/adr`), kotoba-repository-roles (same verifier), repo-reality-verify's structural half (as `manifest-reality-check`).

**Ported here (1).** west-pin-verify's policy job → `gates/west-pin-policy-check.cljs`. Its second job (server-side reachability of changed pins) is deliberately not ported: it needs GitHub API auth, which invariant 3 forbids on a node — and it is already operator-side, since tick.cljs runs `verify-west-pins.cljs` before every CD pin advance. What was missing was only the auth-free policy layer, which nothing was running.

**Portable, not yet ported (9).** foundation-deps-verify, public-runtime-owners, fleet-projection-verify, istore-freeze-check, tree-collapse-guard, clj-wgsl-parity, cloud-itonami-live-observe, annex-custody-verify (needs `git-annex` provisioned via brew rather than apt), and the full-scope docs-edn check. Each is a thin gate script plus one gates.edn line.

**Blocked on their own breakage — decision 4 applies.** Measured directly, with real exit codes: `check-foundation-deps.cljs` rc=1, `90-docs/repo-reality/ci_gate.cljs` rc=1, `verify-public-runtime-owners.mjs` rc=1. Only `west-pin-guard-policy-test.cljs` passed (rc=0), which is why it is the one ported. These invariants are genuinely violated right now; nobody has seen them since Actions stopped. Fix, then register.

*(Method note: the first attempt at that measurement read `$?` after a pipe into `tail`, so every script looked like rc=0. The fleet-ci README's own invariant — do not trust exit 0 — applies to the person doing the survey too.)*

**Write/bot workflows, need care (2).** repo-maturity-regenerate and production-gates-bot mutate the repo rather than checking it. fleet-ci has a landing path (branch + contents PUT + server-side merge, never direct push to main) so they fit, but a bot that commits on every tick is a different risk class from a gate and should be opt-in per repo.

**Cannot move (2).**
- `codeql-analysis` — `github/codeql-action` is a hosted GitHub service. A self-hosted CodeQL CLI is possible but is a separate project with its own licensing question. Options: accept no SAST, or adopt a scanner that runs as a plain binary on a node.
- `jupyterlite` — the *build* is portable, but it ends in `actions/upload-pages-artifact` and deploys to GitHub Pages, which is a GitHub product. Moving it means publishing somewhere else (Cloudflare Pages is already used in this workspace).

## Why the fleet architecture is the right target

Worth stating because it is not obvious from outside: the fleet does things Actions did not.

- Receipts are **signed** into an append-only ledger (`manifest/fleet-ci.edn`), with a weak-permission key that can only append — it holds neither pin-advance nor governance quorum.
- Green **advances the west pin** through server-side verification, so CI and CD are one path.
- `exit 0` is structurally distrusted: extraction success and a test summary are both asserted, after a real false-pass incident where a `clojure` that dropped to a REPL returned 0.
- Nodes hold **no credentials**; the operator fetches private trees and streams tarballs over ssh stdin.

## Retirement, 2026-07-30

`.github/` is deleted from this superproject: 16 workflows plus the now-orphaned
`codeql-config.yml`. The directory held nothing else.

Two findings made this safe rather than merely requested:

- **There are no required status checks.** `GET /branches/main/protection` returns
  403 — branch protection needs GitHub Pro or a public repo, and this is neither.
  So no check could ever have blocked a merge here, and deleting a workflow cannot
  strand a PR waiting on a status that will never arrive. It also retires item 2 of
  the earlier plan: switching required checks to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*` is not
  possible on this plan, so fleet commit statuses are informational for this repo.
- Deletion is reversible. Every workflow stays in git history; this removes the red
  lights and the misleading appearance of coverage, not the recipes.

### Disposition of all 16

| workflow | disposition |
|---|---|
| west-pin-verify | policy half **on the fleet**; API half already operator-side in tick.cljs |
| kotoba-repository-roles | **on the fleet** (same verifier, rc=0, 23 repos verified) |
| docs-edn-verify | docs half **on the fleet** for `90-docs/adr`; full scope still red |
| foundation-deps-verify | invariant **broken** (rc=1) — fix before registering |
| repo-reality-verify | invariant **broken** (rc=1); structural half already on the fleet |
| public-runtime-owners | invariant **broken** (rc=1) |
| istore-freeze-check | needs a **commit range**, not a tree — belongs operator-side |
| tree-collapse-guard | compares HEAD against HEAD^ — same, operator-side |
| fleet-projection-verify | portable, needs a clone of kagami at pin |
| clj-wgsl-parity | portable but heavy (python + west + full west update) |
| cloud-itonami-live-observe | portable, clones loop-system-dynamics |
| annex-custody-verify | portable once `git-annex` is provisioned by brew, not apt |
| repo-maturity-regenerate | **mutates** the repo — bot, opt-in per repo, not a gate |
| production-gates-bot | **mutates** the repo — same |
| codeql-analysis | **capability removed** — hosted service, no fleet equivalent |
| jupyterlite | **capability removed** — build is portable, the Pages deploy is not |

The adr-ledger half of docs-edn-verify was deliberately **not** ported: CLAUDE.md
retires that mechanism per ADR-2607257000 and says not to reintroduce those
scripts. Its check passing (rc=0) is not a reason to give a retired mechanism a
new home.

### What this removes, honestly

`codeql-analysis` was the only SAST in this repo. It has not executed since the
billing stop, so nothing is lost today, but the workflow will not come back on its
own if billing is restored. Replacing it means either accepting no SAST or adopting
a scanner that runs as a plain binary on a node.

`jupyterlite` published to GitHub Pages. Its last run was 2026-06-12 and also
failed, so whatever is served there is already stale. Moving it means choosing a
different publish target; Cloudflare Pages is already used in this workspace.

Neither is a decision this work should make silently, so both are named here as
capability removed pending an owner call.

### Scope

**This superproject only.** 2288 of the 3527 checked-out child repos carry their
own workflows. The evidence gathered here is specific to com-junkawasaki (plus
recent kotoba-lang failures); gftdcojp, etzhayyim, network-awai, cloud-itonami and
jk-luxury were not measured. A sweep of 2288 repos is not something to do on
evidence from one org.

## Live verification, 2026-07-30 — the fleet tick cannot complete

Ran the fleet for real after retiring Actions. **The premise of that retirement was
half wrong and this is the correction.**

### What works

The gate mechanism is sound, proved on real hardware rather than inferred:

- filtered tree shipped over ssh stdin to `asher` (mac mini, Darwin arm64): 1988
  `.edn` / 2142 `.edn+.cljs` / 2001 `.edn+.yml` files selected by pathspec, tarball
  9.7 MB, extract OK
- `west-pin-policy-check.cljs` executed on the node: `3 cases OK`, sentinel
  `FLEET-CI-EXIT: 0`
- nodes reachable with `npx` available: asher, judah, levi, simeon, joseph, benjamin

### What does not work

**`scripts/fleet-ci/tick.cljs` cannot complete a tick on this machine.** It dies
before any gate runs:

    no such item: fleet-agent-murakumo-ci-tip
    batch 0 ci-verify exit 1 (4s)
    ERROR no receipt produced for batch 0

The signing identity `gates.edn` names is not provisioned here — the kagi item is
absent and so is the PEM the plist expects (`~/.gftd/fleet-ci-signer-tip.pem`).
A targeted lookup of the one other documented name found `fleet-agent-murakumo-ci`
present, but `gates.edn` says that is a **different machine's identity** and that
keys are not copied between machines, so pointing at it would break the design
rather than fix it.

**The receipts in the ledger did not come from this code.** Six landed today
between 02:02 and 04:00, signed by `did:key:z6MkhKgm9LbcxSN5uVDBJAhZ3B7s31MHXfS2omgkmCnWu3Ve`.
The loaded LaunchAgent is `com.gftd.fleet-ci-murakumo-tick`, not the repo's
`com.gftd.fleet-ci-tip-tick`, and it runs `~/.gftd/run-fleet-ci-murakumo-tick.cljs`
— an out-of-repo script of 209 lines with a **hardcoded repo list that never reads
gates.edn**.

So `gates.edn` exists precisely to replace hardcoding (its own header says the
ADR-2607178000 runner hardcoded 14 repos and nodes), and the hardcoded runner is
still the one executing. The four `root` gates — docs-edn, repository-roles,
manifest-reality, and the west-pin policy gate added today — are **not in the
running rotation.**

### Consequence for the Actions retirement

Actions was retired because it executes nothing, which remains true and measured.
But the fleet plane that was supposed to be carrying these invariants is not
carrying them either. Restoring Actions would restore nothing, since its jobs do
not start. The honest position: **this superproject's real CI coverage is whatever
the 209-line hardcoded runner checks, and nothing else.**

### Also found and fixed: scratch retention cannot keep up

`asher` was at **100% disk, 117 MiB free**, with 20 GB under `/tmp/fleet-ci` across
474 directories — and **zero of them older than one day**, so the prune in
`gate-command` (`-mtime +1`) could never reclaim anything. A full node fails at
`tar` with `No space left on device`, so its gates die before running.

Pruned entries untouched for 60+ minutes (no gate was in flight; the only live nbb
process was an unrelated murakumo service): 20 GB to 89 MB, 117 MiB to 20 GiB free.
Then changed the policy from `-mtime +1` to `-mmin +120`, which bounds usage to
about two hours of production instead of a day, with a 4x margin over the 30-minute
gate timeout. Also added `-mindepth 1`: the original expression matched the base
directory itself.

## Closing state, 2026-07-30 06:5x

Fleet CI is live. Receipts landed at 06:41:38 and 06:53:04 under
`fleet-ci/tip-verify/v1`, signed by the identity enrolled in this session
(`murakumo-ci-tip-25mbair`). Before it, the repo tick could not complete at all —
it died on a missing signer before running a single gate.

Verified at `origin/main`, not against the commits this work created:

    docs plane   md=76 edn=1859 parse-errors=17 not-tx=7 split-strings=1
                 path-md-refs=7 source-format-residue=0
    fleet gates  4 root gates execute on the mac minis; docs-edn fails on a real
                 defect, the other three pass
    sweeper      LaunchAgent loaded, ran unattended, threshold and idempotence
                 both observed in its own log

What moved this session: parse-errors 22 to 17, split-strings 4 to 0, ~186 GiB
reclaimed across ten nodes, GitHub Actions retired, and the fleet tick unblocked.

### The treadmill is real and measurable

split-strings is back to **1** — `2607300500-compression-codec-plane…` acquired the
same unescaped-quote defect after this session cleared all four. That is the third
instance today of a document breaking within hours of the class being fixed, and it
is the strongest argument for the ratchet recommended above: instance repair does
not hold, and nothing currently fails a build when it regresses.

### Commit SHAs from this session no longer resolve

Seven of the superproject commits made here are not ancestors of `origin/main`
though the objects still exist locally — history was rewritten by something else
during the session. **The content survived**: every file and every edit was checked
individually and is present, including the escapes in
`2607274000-marketplace-seven-actors-one-ref.edn` (verified by parsing it: all keys
keyword, body 6,299 chars, unchanged).

Recorded because a future reader searching for those SHAs will not find them, not
because work was lost. A first pass at this check reported the escapes as missing —
that was a broken shell grep pattern, not a broken file, and the difference is worth
stating so the next person does not re-raise the same false alarm.

## Remaining work, in order

0. **Blocking, before anything else: give this machine a signer identity.** Enroll
   `fleet-agent-murakumo-ci-tip` (or place the PEM the plist expects at
   `~/.gftd/fleet-ci-signer-tip.pem`, the path ADR-2607178000 §3 permits) and grant
   it in `fleet-agents.edn`. Until then `tick.cljs` dies before any gate runs and
   none of the four root gates execute. Do not repoint `gates.edn` at
   `fleet-agent-murakumo-ci` — that is another machine's identity.
1. **Decide which runner is authoritative.** `~/.gftd/run-fleet-ci-murakumo-tick.cljs`
   is what actually produces receipts and it ignores `gates.edn`; the repo's
   `tick.cljs` reads `gates.edn` and cannot sign. One of them has to become the
   single plane, or the data-driven matrix stays decorative.
2. Fix the three failing invariants above, then register them as gates (decision 4).
3. Switch branch-protection required checks to `fleet-ci/murakumo/*`. Until this happens nothing is enforced, whichever plane runs.
4. Decide codeql and jupyterlite.
5. Issue the two fine-grained PATs so the LaunchAgent stops skipping (GitHub Web UI, human step) — otherwise ticks only run from an interactive session.
6. Refresh `scripts/fleet-ci/README.md`, which still lists PR-head verification as not-done.

## 2026-08-05 — enforced repo-wide, on owner instruction

Owner: 「github は使わない、murakumo.cloud の cdci, workflow を使う」. Until this
date the decision was a direction with one ported gate; leaving Actions *enabled*
everywhere meant a repository could still show a green check that stood for
nothing. It is now enforced by disabling Actions per repository, which is the
strongest form available with the credentials this workspace has.

What the credentials actually allow (measured, not assumed):

| operation | scope needed | available? |
|---|---|---|
| edit/delete `.github/workflows/*` | `workflow` | **no** — push and Contents API both refused; the API answers **404**, not 403, which reads as 「repo missing」 |
| disable Actions for one repo | `repo` | **yes** |
| disable Actions for a whole org | `admin:org` | **no** |

So workflow files stay on disk as inert artefacts, and the org-level switch is
out of reach; the sweep is per-repo. `scripts/github-actions-disable-sweep.cljs`
does it — resumable via `~/.gftd/gh-actions-sweep-state.edn`, and it leaves a
400-call rate-limit reserve so fleet-ci's own ticks keep running while it sweeps.

**GitHub code search cannot be used to find the targets.** Asked for
`org:kotoba-lang path:.github/workflows` it returned **1**, while
`kotoba-lang/compiler` alone has 4 workflows; `network-awai` returned 0 and
actually has 11 repos with workflows. The index is too incomplete to scope a
sweep from, so the script enumerates every repository and asks
`GET /repos/{o}/{r}/actions/workflows` directly.

The rule now lives in CLAUDE.md (`CI/CD は murakumo fleet。GitHub Actions を
使わない`), including the fleet-gate constraint that bit hardest in practice:
nodes reach only the tailnet, `ship-git-deps!` carries git deps but **not maven
ones**, so a repo whose tests need a maven library cannot use `:jvm-test` — check
that dependencies resolve on a node before choosing the gate kind, and leave the
library-dependent assertions in the repo's local contract."
